  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "Adjectives in Spanish must agree in number and gender with the nouns they modify. When a noun is plural, the adjective also takes a plural form, typically by adding -s to adjectives ending in a vowel or -es to adjectives ending in a consonant. For example, 'Es muy caro' (It is very expensive) becomes 'Son muy caros' when referring to a plural masculine noun. Similarly, 'Es muy vieja' becomes 'Son muy viejas' in the plural."
    }
